          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Involvement and Exile</strong><br>\nA committed intellectual, Hugo's political views led to a falling out with Napoleon III, resulting in his exile to the Channel Islands. During this period, he wrote some of his best work, including \"Les Misérables.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Les Misérables\"</strong><br>\nPublished in 1862, \"Les Misérables\" is considered one of the greatest novels of the 19th century. It tackles themes of injustice, revolution, and the quest for redemption and has been adapted into numerous films and a successful musical.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Political Stance</strong><br>\nAfter returning to France, Hugo became involved in politics, advocating for social justice, universal suffrage, and the abolition of the death penalty. He was elected to the National Assembly and the Senate.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
